        "abstract": "Systematic reuse and variability management is crucial for large-scale, long-living systems with changing requirements such as IEC 61499 control software. However, in this domain, systems exist and evolve over decades and reuse is typically only done by pursuing ad-hoc strategies like Clone-&-Own. As the number of variants and their complexity grows, manual management of control software variants becomes infeasible. Adopting a systematic reuse approach such as Software Product Lines can help. To re-engineer existing variants into a Software Product Line, a variability mining approach can be used, which identifies commonalities and differences among system variants and supports building variability models and reusable assets. One such approach for IEC 61499 control software is the open-source tool Varflix, which currently builds a variability model based on analyzing existing system variants. In this paper, we present an extension to this tool by introducing the generation of delta modeling artifacts. Delta models define transformation operations to enable deriving variants by selecting a set of features. With this extension a complete reusable platform can be built from Clone-&-Own systems semi-automatically allowing to apply systematic reuse. We apply the approach to a set of existing case studies to evaluate the feasibility of our approach.",
